    /// <summary>
    /// Get Content Statistics. Returns a single aggregate of reach, engagement, value, and
    /// creator counts over the **same content set*; that `content.list` would return for an
    /// identical query. The `post_count` is the number of posts in that whole set, across
    /// every page the list would return, and each other total is the sum over those same
    /// posts. `content.list` never counts the full set: its `item_count` is only the number
    /// of rows in the page it returned, so `post_count` here is the only total available.
    /// This accepts every filter `content.list` accepts and applies the **same defaults*;
    /// and requirements: every request must scope what it covers with at least one of
    /// `creator_id`, `creator_username`, `search`, `brand_id`, or `external_id` (else
    /// `VALIDATION_ERROR`); brand-only filters and sorts still require `brand_id`.
    /// `order_by` is validated the same as in `content.list` (brand-only sorts still
    /// require `brand_id`) but has no effect on the aggregated totals. Returns a single
    /// totals object (`item_count: 1`), not paginated, and echoes the resolved date window
    /// and `brand_id` in `applied_filters`. ### Dates `content.statistics` takes the same
    /// four date parameters as `content.list` and applies them identically. `posted_after`,
    /// `posted_within_hours` (1 to 8760) and `posted_within_days` (1 to 365) define the
    /// start of the window; `posted_before` defines the end. A request with no date
    /// parameter covers the last 7 days. A request carrying more than one start parameter
    /// is valid, and the first present in the order `posted_after`, `posted_within_hours`,
    /// `posted_within_days` is applied; `applied_filters` reports that one. `posted_after`
